# Upscale probe (rename/copy to GP_probe_scale.txt in the game directory to enable)

What it does
------------
Watches every fullscreen draw (vertexCount 3/4/6) and records any whose
**input SRV is smaller than its output RTV** — that signature is an upscale pass.
It also reads back **one** of those passes (the one that reads the internal
render size or writes the back buffer) and reports the **colour space** of its
input, because that decides whether FSR1's EASU can be used as-is.

It changes nothing (pure observation), so it is safe to leave on while playing.

Why
---
Arma renders at renderWidth/renderHeight (Arma3.cfg) and then stretches to the
backbuffer. A spatial upscaler (FSR1 / NIS) can only be inserted at that stretch,
so we need to know:
  1. does a single PS pass do the stretch?
  2. what is its shader (size + crc) so GP_replace.txt can target it?
  3. is the input really a lower-resolution SRV?
  4. is that input already display-referred [0,1], or still HDR scene-linear?
     (EASU requires the former; if it is HDR you must tonemap first.)

How to use
----------
1. Copy this file to the game directory as `GP_probe_scale.txt`.
   The probe is read at startup and re-read every 60 frames, so the file may also
   be dropped in mid-session. Shader recording is NOT gated on this file (that
   was an older build): every pixel shader created after the proxy loads is
   recorded.
2. In game, set Sampling (分辨率采样) to 70% or 50% — otherwise the input and
   output are the same size and nothing will match.
3. Play a few seconds. The proxy rewrites `GP_upscale.txt` every 300 frames.
4. To force a report right now: create an empty `GP_upscale_dump.txt` beside the
   exe. The report is rewritten once per second for as long as that file exists,
   so you do not have to wait or quit the game.
5. Read `GP_upscale.txt` next to the game exe.

Reading the report
------------------
  fullscreen_draws           total fullscreen quads seen
  upscale_candidates_hits    draws whose input < output
  distinct=N/128             candidate table occupancy (128 slots; see below)
  prio                       priority: 2 = reads the internal render size or
                             writes the back buffer. **The pass you want is the
                             prio-2 row whose output is the back buffer.**
  input(SRV) -> output(RTV)  e.g. 1344x756 -> 1920x1080  <-- candidate
  psLen / psCrc              the pixel shader, ready for GP_replace.txt
  via                        Draw / CopyResource / CopySubresourceRegion / Resolve

The table used to be 32 slots, first-come-first-served, and high-frequency UI or
debug passes (e.g. `64x768 -> 1920x1080`, ~95k hits) filled it before the real
stretch (once per frame) could get a slot. It is now 128 slots and evicts the
least interesting entry, so prio-2 rows survive.

Colour-space readback
---------------------
For the prio-2 pass the proxy copies the captured input to a staging texture and
logs one line like:

    [GP] upscale input 00000000 1600x900 fmt=10: min=(0.0100 ...) max=(12.5 ...)
         mean=(0.21 ...) >1=3.4% >4=0.2% n=36864
    [GP] upscale input verdict: HDR / scene-linear -> tonemap before EASU

`fmt=10` is R16G16B16A16_FLOAT. The verdict is "HDR" when any sample exceeds 4.0
or the max exceeds 1.5; otherwise the input is display-referred and EASU can be
dropped in. The readback happens once per (psCrc, size), never per frame.

Whole-frame capture: one launch, everything
-------------------------------------------
After ~240 settled frames the probe writes, once per session:

  GP_passseq.txt   every fullscreen pass of ONE frame, in execution order:
                   idx psCrc psLen in(SRV) out(RTV) fmt src sfmt min..max mean/>1%
                   "src" is the texture that pass sampled, and min..max/mean/>1%
                   are measured on it — i.e. the colour range of every stage.
  GP_shaders.txt   every pixel shader the game created (crc, bytes, cached bytes)
  GP_upscale.txt   all candidate signatures for the session (prio, hits)

The dump is two-phase: the first frame containing "smaller input -> back buffer"
arms it, the next one is captured with the per-pass statistics.

Then turn the bundle into a report:

    python tools\analyze_capture.py "D:\...\Arma 3"

It prints (and can write) the pass chain, marks the final stretch, reports each
stage's colour space, and lists which shaders are replaceable.

To also save a texture (≈12 MB at 1600x900 RGBA16F), create
`GP_upscale_dump_input.txt` before the draw; it lands next to the exe as
`GP_up_in_<crc>_<w>x<h>.raw` (packed rows, 4 bpp for 8-bit, 8 bpp for RGBA16F).
Delete the trigger when you are done, or every session adds another file.

If psCrc is non-zero, the report also prints a ready-to-paste GP_replace.txt
line in GP_shader.log. The first field is the ORIGINAL shader size as the game
passed it (use psLen from the report for that field). Note that psLen changes
with the render resolution — a table entry sampled at 1600x900 will not match
after you change Sampling.

If psLen is 0 for a candidate:
  - the shader came from a device/context we do not hook, or the table was
    reset after 8192 distinct shaders (see the proxy log).
  The row is still a real upscale candidate by geometry; psKey identifies it.

If the report says "No fullscreen pass with input < output was seen":
  - Sampling is probably 100% -> lower it and retry, or
  - the stretch does not happen in one PS pass (e.g. done in a VS-only blit or
    by the swapchain / DXGI scaling) -> then FSR1 must be inserted elsewhere,
    and this negative result is itself the answer worth recording.

Note: this probe is independent of GP_probe_mrt.txt and does not touch
presentMode, so it can run alongside the normal injector presets.
